{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/311333-wraith-questions-necropolis","id":311333,"title":"Wraith Questions - Necropolis","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-11-13T08:59:00-08:00","id":311333,"title":"Wraith Questions - Necropolis","content":"The Necropolis were created by Stygia after the 3rd Great Maelstrom, right? With the first Necropolis being London. But before there were Necropolis, there were no Citadels as well? Are Necropolis just political entities (with the main responsibility of collecting Wraiths for the Legions and Stygian forges), and the Citadels its physical headquarters? Did Citadels exist, and then the Necropolis and its social institutions were put in place? A Citadel exists for each Legion, or they all share a Citadel, and these together are called a Necropolis?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-16T11:35:50-08:00","id":313868,"title":"","content":"A &quot;Necropolis&quot; is understood to mean an administrative unit of the Stygian empire. Before that, ghosts still existed in the Shadowlands and likely congregated in cities, especially old cities with a history of having large populations. But they weren't &quot;Necropolises&quot;.<br /> <br /> A &quot;Citadel&quot; is the political, administrative, and social center of the Necropolis. It resembles a fortified medieval city or castle with permanent residents and commerce. A Citadel is most likely the largest and strongest Haunt of the area. Haunt provides protection against maelstroms, but this also means a Citadel is in the area especially desolate and shunned by the living. Citadels have to be large enough to shelter many wraiths. The rest of the Necropolis is simply the Shadowlands within the administrative district. Just like castles would provide protection which caused people to congregate there and become de facto towns, once a Citadel is established, a lot of the local wraithly population will seek to go there and live outside its walls.<br /> <br /> A Citadel was likely constructed purposefully by the Hierarchy to serve as its administrative center. It has to move into a large enough Haunt and then fortify it with darksteel girders and other &quot;solid&quot; materials like soulforged bricks. A &quot;Citadel&quot; is not necessarily one building, but like a modern military fort has many buildings to serve different needs. There may be a barracks, perhaps multiple per Legion, but there will also be a civil administrative center, an official forum/marketplace, the area where the Anacreons gather, a forge for the Artificers, the official Tempest Port, and lots of other areas. Some of these functions may be in one large building subdivided, or be a building of its own. It'll depend on the size of the wraithly population, how long a Necropolis has existed there, its importance to Stygia, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-17T08:59:43-08:00","id":314560,"title":"","content":"Thank you very much for the answer! But did Citadels (as in big Haunt fortified by soulsteel) exist in Stygian controlled cities in the Shadowlands before the establishment of the Necropoli as political institutions after the 3rd G. Maeltrom? Say I want to set a story in an European city in the Middle Ages. There were Citadels analogs then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-17T10:04:33-08:00","id":314663,"title":"","content":"If anything, there were probably Stygian outposts, analogous to Roman forts, housing mostly Legionnaires. It can be safely assumed that Stygia had some sort of presence in their &quot;territories&quot; prior to the 3rd Great Maelstrom and the construction of the Citadels, more likely militarily than bureaucratic.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-17T22:11:25-08:00","id":315082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/311333-wraith-questions-necropolis?p=314560#post314560\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But did Citadels (as in big Haunt fortified by soulsteel) exist in Stygian controlled cities in the Shadowlands before the establishment of the Necropoli as political institutions after the 3rd G. Maeltrom? Say I want to set a story in an European city in the Middle Ages. There were Citadels analogs then?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that was ever established in canon, but there's no reason why there couldn't be something like that established earlier. If it did, it wouldn't be anywhere near as large or busy or complex as Necropolis Citadels are now, but the Hierarchy would likely have some reason to have some presence in the Shadowlands as Nothing posted. The Tempest is said to have outposts and forts along the byways so the Shadowlands could as well. Perhaps not to serve as an administrative area, but a place for Ferrymen, Legionnaires, and other officials to rest or do business. There is nothing wrong if you do, and nothing wrong if you don't. Do what makes most sense/fun in your game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-17T22:42:16-08:00","id":315087,"title":"","content":"One thing I wonder about is maelstroms. Before GM3 surely there were lesser maelstroms that wraiths would need to seek shelter from. So those places might not be citadels in the modern sense, but like the peasants who needed the walls of a castle to protect them from invaders, wraiths would need a place to go to protect them from maelstroms and, perhaps, spectre invasions.<br /> <br /> It was just that the government of Stygia was more centralized then, with a great deal of the power in the hands of Charon and his Deathlords. If anacreons existed, they were probably just a military rank a step below the Deathlord, as opposed to the political ruling council that they serve in Necropoli.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-18T08:34:38-08:00","id":315422,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the answers, people.<br /> <br /> I have some follow-up questions: is there anything official on the Norse afterlife before Stygia came to power in the Scandinavian region? All the Ferrymen were Hellenic, right? But, like Anubis, there were other similar figures analog to the Ferrymen in other cultures? Or is Anubis the only one mentioned? Are psychopomp slavic or celtic entities mentioned somewhere, for instance?<br /> <br /> Also, when do you think the cultures and peoples surrouding the Greco-Roman culture in which the Stygian Republic/Empire is based were assimilated? In the beginning of the Stygian history, or following in the footsteps of Rome?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-19T19:42:24-08:00","id":316879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/311333-wraith-questions-necropolis?p=315422#post315422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have some follow-up questions: is there anything official on the Norse afterlife before Stygia came to power in the Scandinavian region? All the Ferrymen were Hellenic, right? But, like Anubis, there were other similar figures analog to the Ferrymen in other cultures? Or is Anubis the only one mentioned? Are psychopomp slavic or celtic entities mentioned somewhere, for instance?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think there is anything official, but I always thought that pretty much all the mythological afterlife locations were Far Shores, and any of the psychopomps were the names of Ferrymen. Among the Norse, there are possibly four Far Shores - Valhalla (where Odin received the heroic dead), Folkvangr (where Freya received the heroic dead), Niflheim (where Hel received the dead), and Aegir's Hall (where the drowned went). Ferrymen would be Hermod and Farbauti. I assume the Valkyries are more like Reapers than Ferrymen, but you could use some of their names too.<br /> <br /> I don't think Wraith meant to exclude any of the other mythological pantheons. They only mentioned the most obvious ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, when do you think the cultures and peoples surrouding the Greco-Roman culture in which the Stygian Republic/Empire is based were assimilated? In the beginning of the Stygian history, or following in the footsteps of Rome? </div> </div> </div>Yes. Stygia pretty much includes all of the dead not only from Europe and North America, but also the Islamic lands. North Africa, Middle East and even Persia all fall under it. Lots of European pagans too. Wraith mentions that many of the barbarian wraiths were the first Renegades who attacked Stygia during the First Great Maelstrom when the Western Roman Empire fell. Whether all the wraithly dead outside the Roman Empire were Renegades isn't stated. I think the ST can decide whatever it is he wants to use. Personally, I would hold that a lot of the wraiths outside Rome were part of Stygia or at least would become part of Stygia. Most of those barbarian renegades were more likely Spectres or pawns of Spectres.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}